<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alghero</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as L'Alguer</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Alghero is a port on the north-west coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. Taken by the Crown of Aragon in 1354 and resettled with Catalans, it has kept a Catalan dialect, still spoken by part of its population, and Catalan Gothic buildings.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alghero.jpg" alt="Alghero" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gianni Careddu · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alghero_-_Panorama_(02).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The area has been settled since prehistoric times: the Ozieri culture was present in the 4th millennium BCE, and the Nuragic civilisation settled around 1,500 BCE. By the 8th century BCE Phoenicians had arrived, and the metalworking town of Sant'Imbenia, with a mixed Phoenician and Nuragic population, traded with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etruscans">Etruscans</a>. The medieval name derives from Aleguerium, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> word for a stagnation of algae. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/doria-family">Doria family</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> built the first historical nucleus on an empty stretch of coast between 1102 and 1112, and the Dorias ruled the fortified port for centuries, apart from a year under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Pisa</a> in 1283 and 1284.</p><p>The Crown of Aragon took the town in 1354 at the behest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-iv-of-aragon">Peter IV of Aragon</a>, who promoted its colonisation with families from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mallorca">Majorca</a>, Catalonia and Aragon. They were granted privileges and replaced the original population, some of whom were sent to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberian Peninsula</a> and Majorca as slaves. In the early 16th century Alghero received papal recognition as a bishopric and the status of King's City. Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> rule lasted until 1702. In 1720 Alghero, with the rest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>, passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-savoy">House of Savoy</a>, which began a policy of Italianization. A famine in 1821 led to a revolt that was bloodily suppressed, and the town was demilitarised at the end of that century.</p><p>In the Fascist era part of the surrounding marshes was reclaimed and the suburbs of Fertilia and Santa Maria La Palma were founded. Alghero was bombed in 1943 and its historic centre was heavily damaged. Malaria in the countryside was finally overcome in the 1950s, and the town has since become a tourist destination.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Alghero has a population of 41,765, which makes it the fifth-largest city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>. Algherese, the Catalan dialect introduced by the colonists, has been officially recognised as a minority language, and since 1997 national and regional laws have granted a right to use it in the city. Its speakers call the town Barceloneta, little <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a>. The old town keeps Catalan medieval architecture, and a book of 1888 by Eduardo Toda y Güell on this Catalan population restarted the connection between Catalonia and Alghero.</p><p>Ramparts begun in the 13th century on the Genoese system were restored by the Catalans, and most of the present walls were built in the 16th century under Ferdinand the Catholic, with seven towers and three forts. The Torre del Portal was built in 1360 at the expense of the Jewish community, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> stayed in the Palazzo D'<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albi">Albis</a> in October 1541. The cathedral was begun in 1570 and consecrated in 1730, with Catalan Gothic chapels beneath later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> work. The local red coral gives the coast the name Riviera del Corallo.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Alghero” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The conquests of James I spread the Catalan language and settlers to Valencia and the Balearic Islands, and the Catalan dialect of <b>Alghero</b> in Sardinia survives today.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crown-of-aragon">Crown of Aragon · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The royal towns, above all Cagliari, Sassari, <b>Alghero</b>, Iglesias and Oristano, enjoyed charters and their own councils.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-sardinia">Kingdom of Sardinia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Through that expansion Catalan language and law reached Valencia, the Balearic Islands and the town of <b>Alghero</b> in Sardinia, where a Catalan dialect is still spoken.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-catalonia">Principality of Catalonia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Off <b>Alghero</b> in northwestern Sardinia, the allied fleet of the Crown of Aragon and the Republic of Venice crushed the fleet of the Republic of Genoa, capturing most of it.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-27">27 August in history</a></li></ul></section>
